      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you put a treadmill on carpet?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and most home treadmills spend their lives on carpet without trouble. The three real problems are carpet fibres drawn into the motor, restricted airflow underneath the machine, and a soft base that lets the frame settle unevenly and throws the belt off track. A dense rubber mat solves the first two and helps with the third.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a mat under a treadmill on carpet?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. On carpet a mat is doing a different job from on a hard floor — it is a barrier against fibres and dust reaching the motor, and it spreads the load so the frame does not sink into the pile unevenly. Choose a dense rubber mat rather than a soft foam one, because foam compresses under the machine and makes the stability problem worse.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will a treadmill damage my carpet?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It will leave permanent compression marks under the feet, and on a folding machine the wheels can wear a track in the pile. Neither is avoidable entirely, but a mat spreads the load across a wider area and largely prevents the deep indentations that will not recover. Deep pile carpet marks more visibly than low pile.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does carpet make a treadmill belt go off-centre?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can, and it is a common cause of a belt that will not stay centred. A treadmill frame is long and slightly flexible, and carpet compresses at different rates under different feet, which twists the frame and steers the belt. If your belt drifts again within days of every adjustment, check the base before touching the rollers again.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does carpet make a treadmill quieter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Slightly, for airborne noise in the same room. It does very little for the impact noise transmitted through the structure to the room below, which is what neighbours and downstairs family members actually hear. A dense rubber mat does more for impact noise than carpet does, and neither fully solves a first-floor installation.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should I clean under a treadmill on carpet?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Vacuum around and under the machine monthly, and clean inside the motor hood every six months with the machine unplugged. Carpet sheds continuously, the motor fan draws that lint in, and a coating of fibre on the motor and controller board acts as insulation, trapping the heat those components need to shed.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
